  "default": {
    "status": "ok",
    "statusLabel": "Pays back quickly",
    "headline": "Saves about $426 a year; pays back in about 3.2 years",
    "explain": "With U.S. average prices (17.2¢ per kWh), a 50-gallon heat pump costs about $138 a year to run against $564 for your electric tank. Installed, it costs about $2,947 against $1,579 for a new electric tank: $1,368 more. Over a 13-year life that nets about $4,170. ENERGY STAR advises spaces that stay between 40 and 90 °F year-round with enough air around the unit, so the savings shown should hold.",
    "formula": "Hot water: 55 gal a day, 120 °F setting, 58.0 °F incoming water\nElectric tank: 3,271 kWh × $0.1723 = $564/yr (UEF 0.93)\nHeat pump: 801 kWh × $0.1723 = $138/yr (UEF 3.80)\nYearly saving = $564 − $138 = $426\nHeat pump installed (50-gallon heat pump, Installation labor, Permit, haul-away and disposal of the old unit) = $2,947\nNew electric tank installed (like for like) = $1,579\nExtra cost = $2,947 − $1,579 = $1,368\nPayback = extra ÷ yearly saving = 3.2 years\nNet over 13 years = $426 × 13 − $1,368 = $4,170\nLocation: garage or basement that stays above 40 °f all year (no change)",
    "table": {
      "caption": "Heat pump vs a new electric tank, typical installed prices",
